From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: sunxi: say what the power-source handler did

License: GPL-2.0 (driver patch).

`pinctrl-sunxi-a733-pf-io-voltage-power-source`'s handler writes GPIO_POW_VAL_SET_CTL and returns 0. On silicon that is
indistinguishable from three different outcomes, and two board sessions
(2026-07-29 and 2026-08-02) ended without being able to tell them apart:

  - the property never reached the driver     -> no line at all
  - the handler refused it (desc gate / bank) -> "refused, ..."
  - the handler wrote it                      -> the before/after read-back

Project rule: diagnostics are on by default until the hardware proves the fix,
and the lines stay afterwards. `pinctrl-sunxi-a733-pf-io-voltage-power-source` shipped twice with none, and both times
the only evidence was that the register still read its reset default,
which is consistent with "never ran" AND with "ran, the switch failed, and the
driver restored 3.3 V". One dev_dbg separates them; a third board cycle spent
guessing does not.

The read-back is the point, not the print. Writing a register is not evidence
that the bit changed -- the value is re-read inside the same lock and both
values are reported, so the log line carries the claim instead of asserting it.

Costs nothing when the driver is quiet: CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG is pinned in
A733_CONFIGS, so these are inert until dyndbg="file pinctrl-sunxi.c +p".

Note: the dyndbg target is pinctrl-sunxi.c, where the handler lives, not
pinctrl-sun60iw2.c, which only sets .pf_power_switch.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
@@ -673,22 +673,42 @@
 	unsigned long flags;
 	u32 val;
 
-	if (!pctl->desc->pf_power_switch)
+	u32 before;
+
+	if (!pctl->desc->pf_power_switch) {
+		dev_dbg(pctl->dev,
+			"power-source: pin %u arg %u -- refused, this desc has no pf_power_switch\n",
+			pin, arg);
 		return -ENOTSUPP;
+	}
 
 	/* Only bank PF is SoC-settable through this register. */
-	if (pin / PINS_PER_BANK != PF_BASE / PINS_PER_BANK)
+	if (pin / PINS_PER_BANK != PF_BASE / PINS_PER_BANK) {
+		dev_dbg(pctl->dev,
+			"power-source: pin %u arg %u -- refused, not in bank PF\n",
+			pin, arg);
 		return -ENOTSUPP;
+	}
 
 	raw_spin_lock_irqsave(&pctl->lock, flags);
-	val = readl(pctl->membase + A733_POW_VAL_SET_CTL_REG);
+	before = readl(pctl->membase + A733_POW_VAL_SET_CTL_REG);
+	val = before;
 	if (arg == 1800000)
 		val &= ~BIT(0);
 	else
 		val |= BIT(0);
 	writel(val, pctl->membase + A733_POW_VAL_SET_CTL_REG);
+	/*
+	 * Read back inside the lock. Writing the register is not the claim --
+	 * the claim is that the bit changed, and only a read-back can carry it.
+	 */
+	val = readl(pctl->membase + A733_POW_VAL_SET_CTL_REG);
 	raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&pctl->lock, flags);
 
+	dev_dbg(pctl->dev,
+		"power-source: pin %u arg %u uV -- POW_VAL_SET_CTL %#010x -> %#010x (bit0 %u -> %u)\n",
+		pin, arg, before, val, before & 1, val & 1);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -716,6 +736,14 @@
 		 * MICROVOLTS, same as the device-tree power-source value.
 		 */
 		if (param == PIN_CONFIG_POWER_SOURCE) {
+			/*
+			 * Logged before the call: if this line never appears,
+			 * the property never reached the driver at all, which
+			 * is a different defect from the handler refusing it.
+			 */
+			dev_dbg(pctl->dev,
+				"power-source: seen on pin %u, arg %u uV\n",
+				pin, arg);
 			ret = sunxi_pconf_set_power_source(pctl, pin, arg);
 			if (ret)
 				return ret;
